      "code": "1.2",
      "title": "Acceleration & the Kinematic Equations",
      "concept": {
        "summary": "Acceleration is the rate a velocity changes, in m/s per second. It is a vector, so a negative acceleration does not mean “slowing down” — it means pointing in the negative direction. The four kinematic equations each leave out one quantity, so you choose by asking which one you neither have nor want.",
        "keyIdeas": [
          "An object slows down when a and v point opposite ways, whatever their signs.",
          "Pick the equation by what is missing, not by what looks familiar.",
          "Write your givens with signs before you choose an equation."
        ]
      },
      "priorKnowledge": {
        "items": [
          "Displacement carries a sign; distance does not (S1.1).",
          "Average velocity as Δx over Δt (S1.1).",
          "Solving a quadratic, and knowing when to keep both roots (U0 S0.3)."
        ]
      },
      "directions": "Work every problem in your Hayden-McNeil notebook. Write the givens with signs, then name the equation you chose before you use it.",
